   "text": {
    "L1": "The poem is about kite flying and the child's lively imagination.",
    "L2": "The kites fly like coloured birds. The word “like” shows a simile.",
    "L3": "Both kites and birds are bright and fly high in the sky.",
    "L4": "The child wishes to be small and light as air, so the child could climb on a kite and sail up.",
    "L5": "This is an imagined wish, not something that really happens in the poem.",
    "L6": "The child imagines hearing the songs of the wild wind and seeing the park and rooftops below.",
    "L7": "The people below would stand and stare at the child high in the air.",
    "L8": "“High, high” repeats a word to show that the kite is flying very high.",
    "L9": "“Fly” and “sky” rhyme, while “wild wind” repeats the w sound.",
    "L10": "The poem helps us picture bright kites, a singing wind and a town seen from above."
   },
